  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Anticipate cooler temperatures and periods of valley rain and\n   mountain snow through the weekend.\n\nThe Northern Rockies remain under a very active and unsettled \nweather pattern as the week continues. Periods of valley rain \nshowers (ie: elevations roughly below 4000 feet) are expected each\nday, with mountain passes around and above 4500 feet likely to \nsee minor snow accumulations. While all area mountain passes will \nhave the chance of seeing up to an inch of snow, Lolo Pass and \nLookout Pass will have a higher potential of receiving 2-3 inches \nof new snow both Wednesday and Thursday mornings. If traveling, it\nmay be wise to allow extra time and anticipate wintry conditions.\n\nA low pressure system lingering off the Pacific Northwest coast \non Friday will gradually make its way inland on Saturday and \nSunday, spreading additional valley rain and mountain snow showers\nacross the region. As a result, Halloween may be interrupted by \nperiodic light rain, which looks to increase throughout the day \ninto the evening hours. By Sunday, expect widespread \nprecipitation as the low pressure traverses the area and shifts \neast of the Divide.\n\nThe far extended period beyond Monday (Nov 2) looks much drier \nwith seasonable temperatures.",
